Step 7 Socratic Question: What should I check to avoid the mistake of circular logic, which is drawing conclusions in advance into assumptions? Pause and observe the step carefully: pages 12 to 13. The right triangles divided by the perpendicular bisectors of the first and second sides become SAS congruent, establishing OA = OB = OC. Through this, the two right triangles on the perpendicular line on the third side become RHS congruent, and the chain congruence principle is completed in which the third side is also exactly bisected. What you must remember at this time is that the triangle divided by the circumcenter O is not two opposite triangles, but 'two adjacent right triangles on one side' are congruent (isosceles triangle), and the circumcenter of an obtuse triangle is located outside the triangle.

